※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4391
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4391 LANG_CONSTINIT_MUT

※ A `constinit` variable is not `mut`.  Its initializer is the one thing that
※ writes it, before the program starts, and the pages it is in are read-only
※ once it has; `mut` says anything may write it at any time, which is the one
※ thing it cannot allow.  A variable the program goes on to change is an
※ ordinary `mut` one, which a constructor gives its first value where that has
※ to be worked out.
※
※ Compare: **C++**, where `constinit` says only how a variable is first given
※ its value and `const` beside it is a second, separate word; **D**, where
※ `immutable` alone is the rule and there is no second word to get wrong.

let std := ⎕import("std")

constinit let limit: mut u32 = ⎕narrow(⎕auxv(6) ?? 10u64, ⌜u32⌝) ?? 10u32

@[startup]
fn main(init: std.Init) → u6:
    ⎕narrow(limit, ⌜u6⌝) ?? 0u6
